---
const facts: { label: string; value: string }[] = [
  { label: "Currently", value: "Software Engineering, True Coders bootcamp" },
  { label: "Focus", value: "C# / .NET · full-stack web & mobile" },
  { label: "Education", value: "B.S. Information Technology" },
  { label: "Proof", value: "An app in daily production use" },
];
---

<section
  id="about"
  aria-labelledby="about-heading"
  class="py-20 sm:py-24 lg:py-28"
>
  <header class="mb-12 sm:mb-14">
    <h2
      id="about-heading"
      class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-text sm:text-4xl"
    >
      About
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base text-text-muted sm:text-lg">
      How I got here — and why it makes me a better engineer.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.6fr_1fr] lg:gap-14">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-text-muted sm:text-lg">
      <p>
        I came to software the practical way: by building something that had
        to work. Working in home health &mdash; handling scheduling, billing,
        and mileage tracking &mdash; I watched a field nurse lose hours every
        day to logistics. So I built <span class="text-text">NurseTrack</span>
        to fix it. It has run in production every single day since, planning a
        real nurse's schedule and routes in the field.
      </p>
      <p>
        That project turned a job into a calling. I'm now formalizing the
        self-taught foundation through the
        <span class="text-text">True Coders</span> software engineering
        bootcamp, going deeper on C# and .NET, and re-architecting NurseTrack
        into <span class="text-text">ServeSync</span> &mdash; a multi-tenant
        commercial platform on ASP.NET Core and Azure.
      </p>
      <p>
        The career switch isn't a gap to explain away &mdash; it's the reason I
        build the way I do. Years of solving real operational problems taught
        me to start from the user and the outcome, ship things that hold up
        under daily use, and sweat the details that make software actually
        dependable. I'd rather build one tool people rely on than ten demos
        that never leave localhost.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="lg:pt-1">
      <dl class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-border bg-border">
        {
          facts.map((fact) => (
            <div class="bg-surface p-5">
              <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent">
                {fact.label}
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-1.5 text-base text-text">{fact.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
